          "domain": "information-and-ideas",
          "skill": "central-ideas-and-details",
          "passage": "When the satellite archaeologist Sarah Parcak began applying high-resolution multispectral imagery to a survey of the eastern Nile delta in the early 2010s, the standard expectation among Egyptologists was that the great mounds — the tells visible from the ground — represented essentially the full settlement record of the late Bronze Age period. Parcak's processed images, calibrated against what the team had already excavated at known sites, revealed something different. Subsurface walls and street grids, invisible to surface survey but distinguishable in the multispectral data through their effects on overlying soil moisture, showed up across stretches of cultivated alluvium that had previously been classified as off-site. The implication, in the team's published account, was not that the existing chronology of the period was wrong, but that the density of habitation reflected in the conventional site map had been substantially underestimated.",
