          "explanation": "Two premises do the work. First, the team's mechanistic models, when fitted to the historical cholera data, retain both an environmental and an immune-driven component. Second, the two components together account for substantially more variance in observed cholera incidence than either does alone. The minimum inference the premises license is therefore that any model that isolates one of the two components while excluding the other will explain less of the historical variance than the team's combined models do. Choice A states this measured conclusion.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice B: scope shift — B claims that abandoning mechanistic modeling for purely statistical correlation would recover the team's full results without further assumptions. The premises do not specify what other modeling strategies would or would not produce; they speak only to the variance accounted for by isolating components within the team's mechanistic framework. The inference reaches outside the premises.\n- Choice C: inverse — C claims that an environment-only model would fit the colonial-period data more closely than the team's combined models. This contradicts the second premise, which is that the combined model accounts for more variance than either component alone. C reverses what the passage establishes.\n- Choice D: surface match — D promotes the late-nineteenth-century date to a prerequisite for any modern dynamical-systems analysis. The passage notes that the team draws on records that go back to the colonial reporting period, but it does not claim that earlier data are required for the analysis. D imports a foundational prerequisite the premises do not state.",
