          "passage": "The following text is adapted from Edith Wharton's 1911 novel *Ethan Frome*. The frame narrator, an engineer working through a winter in the New England village of Starkfield, has been gathering scattered remarks about a local resident named Ethan Frome and is summarizing what those remarks add up to.\n\nIt was the village postmaster who first directed my attention to him, with the air of a man making over a familiar object to a stranger. Frome had been a smart fellow once, the postmaster said, and had run the sawmill creditably; he had had ambitions of a kind, and there were people in Starkfield who still thought, if his luck had lain along a different road, those ambitions might have come to something. But the road had not lain that way; and the long winters had set in, and the sawmill had been let go to other hands, and a smashup on the way back from the depot one February night had left him as I now saw him at the corner of Main Street, leaning on a cane and the post of a fence, with his eyes on the snow. The postmaster told me all this without expression, in the tone of a man not so much summing up another's story as explaining a fixture in the landscape.",
