              "label": "Text 2",
              "text": "The ethnobotanist and Potawatomi scholar Robin Wall Kimmerer accepts Cronon's diagnosis of the wilderness ideal as both historically recent and damaging. Where she takes the argument is, however, distinct from Cronon's: rather than working principally in the register of critique, she develops a positive program in which Indigenous practices of reciprocity supply the alternative the critique has cleared space for. Kimmerer's writing draws on Potawatomi traditions of the Honorable Harvest and on her training in plant ecology to argue that humans can themselves be a keystone species in regional ecosystems when bound by gift-based, reciprocal relationships with plants and lands; properly attended to, those relationships generate ecological abundance rather than degradation. The implication, on her account, is not that environmental thought should remain at the level of cultural-construction critique. The displacement Cronon identifies must be answered, she argues, by reconstructing the ethical and ecological practices that the wilderness ideal made invisible — and that work, rather than the critique alone, is what changes how landscapes are actually treated."
