          "skill": "central-ideas-and-details",
          "passage": "Working in the Pacific Northwest in the 1990s and 2000s, the deep-sea ecologist Lisa Levin and her collaborators have used long-term observation along oxygen-minimum-zone gradients to argue that the conventional model of seafloor communities — in which species respond chiefly to depth, substrate type, and temperature — leaves out a determinant of comparable weight. The OMZ communities Levin samples include species whose distributions track dissolved-oxygen concentrations more tightly than they track any of the conventional axes; some of those species are absent from waters that, on temperature and substrate alone, look identical to where they are found. The methodological consequence, in Levin's recent reviews, is that surveys designed only around depth and substrate will systematically underestimate how seafloor communities will reorganize as oxygen-minimum zones expand under continued ocean warming.",
