          "skill": "central-ideas-and-details",
          "passage": "The cultural-studies theorist Stuart Hall, in a series of essays from the late 1970s through the 1990s, argued that what audiences make of a televised news broadcast is determined neither by the broadcaster's intended meaning alone nor by each viewer's personal preferences alone, but by an interaction between them — what he termed the encoding-decoding cycle. The conventional reading of Hall's argument has tended to flatten that cycle into a one-line claim about polysemy: that any media text can be read in many ways. Hall's later restatements were more specific. Audience decodings, he insisted, were not free-form interpretations distributed evenly across all logically possible meanings; they clustered around three positions — dominant, negotiated, and oppositional — whose distribution was itself shaped by social position. The cycle was constrained by structure on both ends, even though the constraints were not deterministic.",
