          "domain": "information-and-ideas",
          "skill": "inferences",
          "passage": "The aerospace engineer Aprille Ericsson has spent more than two decades at NASA's Goddard Space Flight Center designing attitude-control systems — the small reaction wheels and thrusters that hold a spacecraft pointed in a chosen direction during long-exposure imaging. Her writing emphasizes a constraint that distinguishes spaceborne imaging from ground-based imaging: a satellite is constantly responding to small disturbances (atmospheric drag in low orbit, micro-meteoroid impacts, internal vibration from cooling pumps and reaction wheels themselves) that, uncorrected, would smear an image during exposure. Ericsson's design rule of thumb is that a sharp image at a given exposure time requires the attitude-control system to compensate for those disturbances faster than they accumulate. From this it follows that an imaging instrument designed to take longer exposures than its current envelope, without other changes, would ______",
