          "skill": "inferences",
          "passage": "When the literary theorist Linda Hutcheon coined the term \"historiographic metafiction\" in her 1988 study *A Poetics of Postmodernism*, she meant to describe novels that simultaneously install and undermine the historical conventions they invoke — narrating documented events while drawing the reader's attention to the constructed nature of any historical account, including the novel's own. Hutcheon was careful to distinguish this doubleness from two adjacent but different effects: the merely metafictional (a novel that calls attention to its own status as a novel without reference to history) and the merely historical (a novel that reconstructs a documented period without raising questions about how documentation works). On Hutcheon's definition, then, a novel that performs only one of those two operations — even very thoroughly — would ______",
