          "skill": "command-of-evidence-textual",
          "passage": "Charlotte Perkins Gilman's 1892 short story *The Yellow Wall-Paper* is told in a series of journal entries by an unnamed first-person narrator who has been confined, on her physician husband's prescription, to a single upstairs room of a rented colonial house for the summer. A literary critic argues that what the story tracks across its short length is not the narrator's individual breakdown alone but the steady narrowing of the field of objects she is permitted to attend to: the room becomes an enforced stage for a kind of attention the narrator does not herself choose, and the wallpaper finally becomes the only object on which any sustained attention is possible.",
