          "passage": "When the marine biologist Rachel Carson published *Silent Spring* in 1962, the immediate response from the chemical industry's representatives was to attack the book on its rhetorical choices and on what those representatives described as its overstatement of pesticide harms. Several major reviews in the trade press of the early 1960s focused on Carson's prose style and on individual passages they read as alarmist, and at least two formal industry-sponsored rebuttals were circulated within the first year of publication. ______ a 1963 advisory committee convened by President Kennedy reviewed the underlying ecological and toxicological evidence and concluded, broadly, that the substantive scientific case Carson assembled was sound, even where the report's authors acknowledged the rhetorical force of her presentation as a separate matter.",
