          "passage": "In *A Misplaced Massacre* (2013), the historian Ari Kelman tracks the long process through which the 1864 Sand Creek Massacre site in southeastern Colorado was eventually established as a National Historic Site by the National Park Service. The bulk of the book reads as a documentary history of agency review, of legal and jurisdictional negotiation among federal, military, and Cheyenne and Arapaho descendant communities, and of the survey work that finally fixed the site's boundaries. ______ Kelman is consistent in framing the long delay not as a failure of any one party's competence but as a structural consequence of the incompatible understandings that the parties held of what the site itself meant — understandings that no formal review could have reconciled simply by following procedure.",
